window.CV_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An image array has shape (300, 451, 3). Which call turns it into the layout a PyTorch convolution expects?","o":["t.reshape(3, 300, 451)","t.permute(2, 0, 1)","t.view(3, 300, 451)","t.permute(2, 1, 0)"],"a":1,"w":"permute moves the channel axis to the front. reshape and view keep memory order and scramble the channels; permute(2, 1, 0) also swaps height and width."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is torch.tensor([200], dtype=torch.uint8) + 100 ?","o":["300","255","44","An overflow error"],"a":2,"w":"uint8 arithmetic wraps modulo 256: 300 − 256 = 44. It does not saturate at 255 and it does not raise."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which library returns pixels in BGR order by default?","o":["OpenCV","PIL","scikit-image","torchvision’s decode_image"],"a":0,"w":"cv2.imread gives BGR. The pixel (150, 225) of the cat came back as [124, 150, 190] from OpenCV and [190, 150, 124] from the others."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On the Imagenette validation set, which mistake left ResNet-50’s accuracy almost unchanged?","o":["Feeding 0–255 values","Using reshape instead of permute","Normalising twice","Skipping normalisation (values 0 to 1)"],"a":3,"w":"Skipping normalisation cost about one point (87.18% to 86.22%). The other three scored 0.00%, 0.00% and 0.13%."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After the standard ImageNet preprocessing, what range of values should a batch roughly have?","o":["0 to 255","About −2.1 to 2.6","0 to 1","−1 to 1 exactly"],"a":1,"w":"Black in the red channel is (0 − 0.485) / 0.229 = −2.118 and white in the blue channel is (1 − 0.406) / 0.225 = 2.64."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What should the very first training loss of a fresh 10-class classifier be, roughly?","o":["0","About 2.3","About 10","It can be anything"],"a":1,"w":"Near-uniform predictions give cross-entropy ln 10 = 2.303. The measured first loss was 2.41."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does forgetting opt.zero_grad() make training blow up?","o":["The optimiser stops updating","The loss is computed twice","The gradients become zero","backward() adds into .grad , so each step uses the sum of all previous gradients"],"a":3,"w":"Accumulation is by design. Without clearing, steps keep growing: the loss hit 120,332 by step 50 and then NaN."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does F.cross_entropy expect as its first argument?","o":["Raw logits","Softmax probabilities","Log-probabilities from log_softmax","One-hot labels"],"a":0,"w":"It applies log-softmax itself. Passing probabilities softmaxes twice; that run reached 49.7% instead of 52.6%."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An MLP trained with SGD at lr 0.2 ends with NaN loss. What will its test accuracy on balanced 10-class data be?","o":["About 50%","0%","About 10%","Impossible to say"],"a":2,"w":"NaN weights give the same prediction for every image, so it is right for exactly one class in ten: 10.00%."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why overfit one small batch before a long run?","o":["It initialises the weights better","A working model and loop must reach near-zero loss on it; if not, there is a bug, found in seconds","It measures test accuracy","It sets the learning rate automatically"],"a":1,"w":"The MLP reached 100% on 64 images by step 10. A model that cannot memorise 64 images has a bug in the model, the loss or the loop."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In sRGB, which stored value emits half the light of 255?","o":["128","64","About 188","About 220"],"a":2,"w":"sRGB is gamma-encoded. Linear 0.5 converts to 187.5; a stored 128 is only about 0.216 of full light."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which loader applies a JPEG’s EXIF orientation tag by default?","o":["cv2.imread","PIL.Image.open","torchvision.io.decode_jpeg","torchvision.io.decode_image"],"a":0,"w":"OpenCV rotated the 426 × 320 test file to 320 × 426. PIL needs ImageOps.exif_transpose , and torchvision needs apply_exif_orientation=True ."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Re-encoding Imagenette’s own JPEGs at quality 75 gave a higher PSNR than at quality 95. Why?","o":["q75 files are larger","PSNR is measured wrongly","q95 adds noise on purpose","The originals were saved at q75, so the same quantisation table re-produces nearly the same values"],"a":3,"w":"The originals averaged 25.4 KB, exactly the q75 size. Re-quantising with the same table changes almost nothing; q95’s different table moves every coefficient a little."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the greyscale value of RGB (200, 120, 40) with the standard luma weights?","o":["120","About 135","200","About 92"],"a":1,"w":"0.299 × 200 + 0.587 × 120 + 0.114 × 40 = 134.78. The plain mean is 120."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which change cost ResNet-50 the least accuracy on Imagenette?","o":["Rotating 90°","JPEG quality 8","Mirroring left to right","Swapping to BGR"],"a":2,"w":"Mirroring cost nothing (87.32% both ways): the model was trained with horizontal flips. Rotating cost 11 points, q8 12 points, BGR 4.9."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is a point written as (x, y, 1) for geometric transforms?","o":["To store colour","So translation, like rotation and scale, becomes a matrix product","To make the maths use integers","To mark the pixel as valid"],"a":1,"w":"With homogeneous coordinates every affine transform, translation included, is a 3 × 3 matrix, so sequences of transforms compose into one matrix."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You want to rotate an image about its centre (cx, cy). Which matrix, applied to (x, y, 1), does it?","o":["R · T(cx, cy)","T(cx, cy) · T(−cx, −cy) · R","T(−cx, −cy) · R · T(cx, cy)","T(cx, cy) · R · T(−cx, −cy)"],"a":3,"w":"Read right to left: move the centre to the origin, rotate, move back. It matched cv2.getRotationMatrix2D to 7 × 10⁻¹⁵."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which interpolation should you use to resize a segmentation label mask?","o":["Nearest","Bilinear","Bicubic","Antialiased bilinear"],"a":0,"w":"Labels are categories. Blending class 1 and class 3 gives class 2, which is wrong. Nearest only ever copies existing labels."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many point correspondences determine a homography?","o":["2","3","4","9"],"a":2,"w":"H has 9 entries but is defined up to scale, so 8 unknowns; each point pair gives two equations. Four pairs (no three on a line) fix it. An affine transform needs only three."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An image 451 pixels wide is flipped left to right. What does the box (100, 50, 220, 180) become?","o":["(351, 50, 231, 180)","(231, 50, 351, 180)","(100, 50, 220, 180)","(451, 50, 571, 180)"],"a":1,"w":"x maps to W − x, and the corners swap roles so that x1 < x2: (451 − 220, 50, 451 − 100, 180)."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A kernel’s weights sum to 0. What is its output on a perfectly flat region of the image?","o":["The original brightness","255","0","Half the brightness"],"a":2,"w":"Every pixel is the same value v, so the output is v × (sum of weights) = 0. Such kernels (Sobel, Laplacian) respond only to change."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which of these computes a true convolution, flipping the kernel?","o":["cv2.filter2D","scipy.ndimage.convolve","torch.nn.functional.conv2d","All of them"],"a":1,"w":"SciPy’s convolve flips the kernel; it gave exactly the negative of the hand-written Sobel result. OpenCV and PyTorch compute correlation."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many multiplies per pixel does a separable 31 × 31 Gaussian need?","o":["961","31","480","62"],"a":3,"w":"Two 1-D passes of 31 weights each: 62, against 961 for the full 2-D kernel."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An image has 5% of its pixels randomly set to pure black or white. Which filter removed this best?","o":["A 3 × 3 median","A 5 × 5 box blur","A Gaussian blur, σ = 2","A bilateral filter"],"a":0,"w":"Median 3 × 3 reached 33.92 dB; the best averaging filter 28.66 and bilateral 21.37. A median ignores outliers; an average spreads them."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did a 9 × 9 box blur with zero padding make the left edge of the photo dark?","o":["The kernel was not normalised","uint8 overflow","Part of the kernel averaged in zeros from outside the image","The Sobel step removed brightness"],"a":2,"w":"At the leftmost column, 4 of the kernel’s 9 columns lie outside the image, where zero padding supplies black: 68.7 instead of about 124."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why must DataLoader code with workers sit under if __name__ == \"__main__\": on Windows?","o":["It makes the loader faster","It enables pinned memory","Workers are spawned as new processes that import the script, which would otherwise start loaders recursively","PyTorch requires it for CUDA"],"a":2,"w":"On Windows and macOS workers start by spawning, which re-imports the main module. The guard stops that import from re-running the training code."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Decoding the same photo at 1,280 px instead of 320 px took how much longer?","o":["About the same","About 15 times","About 2 times","About 100 times"],"a":1,"w":"6.257 ms against 0.423 ms: decode cost grows roughly with the number of pixels (16 times as many here)."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A loader produces 1,074 images/s and the GPU can train ResNet-18 on 2,857 images/s. Roughly how busy is the GPU?","o":["100%","About 85%","About 60%","About 38%"],"a":3,"w":"The pipeline runs at the slower stage: 1,074 / 2,857 ≈ 0.38. The GPU waits the rest of the time."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which change raised 8-worker throughput from 3,939 to 6,357 images/s?","o":["Returning uint8 batches and normalising on the GPU","Pinning memory","Using batch size 256","Using PIL instead of torchvision"],"a":0,"w":"The workers skip the float conversion and normalisation, and each batch is a quarter of the bytes. Pinning made little difference here."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How do you test whether training is limited by data loading?","o":["Double the learning rate","Check the loss curve","Compare the step rate on data already on the GPU with the real loop, and time waits on the loader","Count the model’s parameters"],"a":2,"w":"ResNet-18 ran 2,857 images/s on synthetic data and 2,813 with the real loader, waiting 3.9% of the time: compute-bound."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Losses & Metrics for Classifiers","f":"p7-losses-and-metrics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does exp(z) / exp(z).sum() return for logits [1000, 0, −5] in float32?","o":["[1, 0, 0]","[NaN, 0, 0]","[0.33, 0.33, 0.33]","An overflow exception"],"a":1,"w":"e^1000 overflows to infinity, and infinity / infinity is NaN. torch.softmax subtracts the max first and returns [1, 0, 0]."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Losses & Metrics for Classifiers","f":"p7-losses-and-metrics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The model called 1,000 images “dog”, and 610 of them were dogs. Which metric is 0.61?","o":["Recall for dog","Accuracy","F1 for dog","Precision for dog"],"a":3,"w":"Precision is the share of predicted positives that are correct. Recall would be the share of real dogs that were found (0.83 here)."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Losses & Metrics for Classifiers","f":"p7-losses-and-metrics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Cats are 10% of a test set. What accuracy does “always answer not-cat” get on the cat-vs-rest task?","o":["90%","10%","50%","0%"],"a":0,"w":"It is right on all 9,000 non-cats and wrong on 1,000 cats: 90%, with a recall of zero. That is the accuracy paradox."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Losses & Metrics for Classifiers","f":"p7-losses-and-metrics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Lowering the threshold of a cat detector usually does what?","o":["Raises precision and recall","Lowers both","Raises recall and lowers precision","Changes neither"],"a":2,"w":"More images are called cats: more real cats are found, and more non-cats are wrongly included."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Losses & Metrics for Classifiers","f":"p7-losses-and-metrics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is cross-entropy used for training instead of accuracy?","o":["It is faster to compute","Accuracy has zero gradient almost everywhere; cross-entropy is smooth and gives a signal on every image","Accuracy cannot be computed on a GPU","Cross-entropy ignores mistakes"],"a":1,"w":"Small weight changes rarely flip a prediction, so accuracy does not change and gives no gradient. Cross-entropy changes smoothly with every probability."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"GPU Memory & Throughput for Images","f":"p8-gpu-memory-and-throughput.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What took most of the memory when training ResNet-50 at batch 256?","o":["The weights","The gradients","The optimiser’s momentum","Activations saved for the backward pass"],"a":3,"w":"Weights, gradients and momentum are about 100 MB each; activations were about 42 MB per image, roughly 10.9 GB of the 11.2 GB peak."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"GPU Memory & Throughput for Images","f":"p8-gpu-memory-and-throughput.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Going from 224 × 224 to 448 × 448 at the same batch size multiplies activation memory by about…","o":["2","4","8","It stays the same"],"a":1,"w":"Activations scale with height × width: (448/224)² = 4. Measured at batch 32: 1,737 MB to 5,812 MB."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"GPU Memory & Throughput for Images","f":"p8-gpu-memory-and-throughput.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which configuration trained ResNet-50 fastest here?","o":["fp32","fp32 + channels-last","bf16 autocast + channels-last","bf16 autocast alone"],"a":2,"w":"1,200 images/s against 928 for bf16 alone, 576 for fp32 and 122 for fp32 with channels-last."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"GPU Memory & Throughput for Images","f":"p8-gpu-memory-and-throughput.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did inference at batch 64 give 14.6× the throughput of batch 1?","o":["At batch 1 the GPU is mostly idle between small kernel launches; a large batch keeps it busy","Batch 64 uses less precision","Batch 1 runs on the CPU","Batching skips layers"],"a":0,"w":"Per-batch overheads are paid once per batch. At batch 1 they dominate: 3.74 ms for one image, 16.4 ms for 64."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"GPU Memory & Throughput for Images","f":"p8-gpu-memory-and-throughput.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A run hits CUDA out of memory. Which fix keeps the same effective batch size?","o":["Lower the resolution","Use a smaller model","Delete the optimiser state","Halve the batch and accumulate gradients over two steps"],"a":3,"w":"Two half-batches with one optimiser step give the same update (for models without batch-statistics layers) at about half the activation memory."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Computer Vision Is","f":"01-what-computer-vision-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which task outputs a class label for every pixel?","o":["Classification","Detection","Semantic segmentation","Retrieval"],"a":2,"w":"A semantic segmenter outputs an (H, W) map of class indices. Detection outputs boxes; classification one label per image."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Computer Vision Is","f":"01-what-computer-vision-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did the ImageNet classifier call the astronaut photo “crash helmet”?","o":["ImageNet’s 1,000 classes do not include “person” or “astronaut”","The image was preprocessed wrongly","The model is too small","The photo is black and white"],"a":0,"w":"A classifier must pick one of its own labels. The detector, trained on COCO, found the person with 0.994."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Computer Vision Is","f":"01-what-computer-vision-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which of the three models was slowest per image on this GPU?","o":["The classifier","The segmenter","All took the same time","The detector"],"a":3,"w":"Faster R-CNN took about 26–33 ms: it scores many candidate regions, refines each box and removes duplicates. The classifier took about 4 ms."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Computer Vision Is","f":"01-what-computer-vision-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The classifier gave “bubble” 45.7% for the Hubble deep field. What does that show?","o":["The model knows galaxies look like bubbles","Softmax picks the least unlikely known class; a high score does not mean the input is in distribution","45.7% means the model is unsure","The image needed a higher resolution"],"a":1,"w":"Probabilities sum to 1 over known classes, so an unknown input still gets a winner, here with more confidence than a real French horn (39.8%)."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Computer Vision Is","f":"01-what-computer-vision-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The segmenter labelled the garbage-truck photo entirely as background. Why?","o":["The truck was too small","Segmenters cannot see vehicles","Its 20 Pascal VOC classes include bus and car but not truck","The threshold was too high"],"a":2,"w":"With no truck class, “background” was its best option. The COCO-trained detector, which has a truck class, found it with 0.999."},{"n":2,"ti":"Convolutions in Depth","f":"02-convolutions-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How many parameters does nn.Conv2d(64, 128, kernel_size=3) have?","o":["1,152","73,856","8,192","589,824"],"a":1,"w":"128 kernels of 3 × 3 × 64 = 576 weights, plus 128 biases: 73,728 + 128."},{"n":2,"ti":"Convolutions in Depth","f":"02-convolutions-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the output width for input 224, kernel 3, stride 2, padding 0, dilation 1?","o":["112","110","111","113"],"a":2,"w":"(224 + 0 − 2 − 1) // 2 + 1 = 110 + 1 = 111. The division rounds down."},{"n":2,"ti":"Convolutions in Depth","f":"02-convolutions-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does im2col trade for speed?","o":["Memory: every patch is copied into a column, about K² times the input size","Accuracy: it approximates the convolution","It needs the kernel to be separable","It only works with stride 1"],"a":0,"w":"It is exact (matched F.conv2d to 10⁻¹⁴) and turns the layer into one matrix multiply, but the columns were 441 MB for a 49 MB input."},{"n":2,"ti":"Convolutions in Depth","f":"02-convolutions-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two conv layers had the same FLOPs. Why was the 3 → 64 layer at 224 × 224 ten times slower per FLOP than the 64 → 64 layer at 56 × 56?","o":["It used a bigger kernel","It ran on the CPU","cuDNN does not support 3 channels","With 3 input channels each output needs few multiply-adds, so moving the large output dominates: it is memory-bound"],"a":3,"w":"27 multiply-adds per output and a huge 224 × 224 × 64 output per image: arithmetic per byte is low, so memory bandwidth sets the speed (6.8 vs 70 TFLOPS)."},{"n":2,"ti":"Convolutions in Depth","f":"02-convolutions-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"ResNet-50’s layer4 has a theoretical receptive field of 427 pixels. How wide was the square holding 90% of the gradient in the trained network?","o":["427 pixels","About 285 pixels","About 32 pixels","The whole image"],"a":1,"w":"The gradient fades from the centre, so the effective receptive field (285) is about two thirds of the theoretical one."},{"n":3,"ti":"Building a CNN","f":"03-building-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A (N, 32, 32, 32) tensor goes through a 2 × 2 max pool. What comes out?","o":["(N, 16, 32, 32)","(N, 32, 32, 32)","(N, 32, 16, 16)","(N, 64, 16, 16)"],"a":2,"w":"Pooling halves height and width and leaves the channel count alone; the convolutions change the channels."},{"n":3,"ti":"Building a CNN","f":"03-building-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Without augmentation the 4.2M-parameter MLP reached 99.4% training accuracy and 58.6% test accuracy. What does that show?","o":["It has enough capacity to memorise, but no structure that generalises across positions","It needed more epochs","The test set is mislabelled","MLPs cannot run on a GPU"],"a":0,"w":"Capacity is not the problem; the inductive bias is. The 308k-parameter CNN generalised to 85.8% on the same unaugmented data."},{"n":3,"ti":"Building a CNN","f":"03-building-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the main practical advantage of a global average pooling head over flatten + linear?","o":["Much higher accuracy","It removes the need for convolutions","It trains without labels","It works for any input size and uses fewer parameters"],"a":3,"w":"Averaging each channel over the whole map gives a fixed-length vector whatever the image size. Here it matched flatten (87.6% vs 88.0%) with 19,200 fewer parameters."},{"n":3,"ti":"Building a CNN","f":"03-building-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A convolution layer is translation…","o":["invariant: its output does not change when the input shifts","equivariant: its output shifts with the input","neither","invariant only with padding"],"a":1,"w":"Shift the input by 3 pixels and the output shifts by 3 (maximum difference 0.0 away from the borders). Invariance comes partly from pooling and the head."},{"n":3,"ti":"Building a CNN","f":"03-building-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Shifting test images 8 pixels cost which model the least accuracy?","o":["The 1,024-wide MLP","The CNN with a flatten head","The CNN with global average pooling","They all lost the same"],"a":2,"w":"86.3% → 67.6% (−18.8 points), against −23.4 for the flatten head and −30.9 for the MLP. Global pooling ignores where features fired."},{"n":4,"ti":"Training a CNN on CIFAR-10","f":"04-training-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Training and test accuracy are both 60% and close together after many epochs. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Overfitting","Underfitting: the model or the optimisation is the limit","A data leak","Perfect training"],"a":1,"w":"No gap means the model is not memorising; low accuracy on both means it cannot fit well yet: more capacity, more epochs or a better learning rate."},{"n":4,"ti":"Training a CNN on CIFAR-10","f":"04-training-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The linear scaling rule says that when the batch size doubles, the learning rate should…","o":["halve","stay the same","double","be multiplied by four"],"a":2,"w":"Twice the images per step and half the steps per epoch; doubling the rate keeps the movement per epoch similar. Here it broke above batch 256."},{"n":4,"ti":"Training a CNN on CIFAR-10","f":"04-training-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is a learning-rate warm-up used at the start of training?","o":["Large early steps on random weights and unsettled BatchNorm statistics can push training somewhere it does not recover from","To save memory","To make the GPU warm up its clock","It is required by SGD"],"a":0,"w":"Early gradients are large and poorly aimed. Starting low and ramping up avoids divergence, which matters most for large learning rates and batches."},{"n":4,"ti":"Training a CNN on CIFAR-10","f":"04-training-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On 5,000 images with no regularisation, test loss rose from 1.34 to 2.29 while test accuracy rose from 53% to 62%. How?","o":["A bug in the loss","The test set changed","Accuracy was measured on training data","More images became correct, while the remaining mistakes became more confident, which cross-entropy punishes heavily"],"a":3,"w":"Loss and accuracy measure different things. Overconfident errors raise cross-entropy even as the count of correct answers grows."},{"n":4,"ti":"Training a CNN on CIFAR-10","f":"04-training-a-cnn.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Going from batch 128 to 2,048 on this GPU changed the time per epoch by about…","o":["16 times faster","10% faster","2 times faster","It became slower"],"a":1,"w":"15.7 s to 14.1 s for 10 epochs: the GPU was already busy at 128. The larger batch bought almost no speed and cost 36 points of accuracy."},{"n":5,"ti":"Data Augmentation","f":"05-data-augmentation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Adding cutout lowered training accuracy from 99.2% to 97.0% and raised test accuracy. Why is that a good sign?","o":["It means training was buggy","Lower training accuracy is always better","The model memorised less and generalised more: the train–test gap shrank","Cutout removes noisy labels"],"a":2,"w":"Regularisation makes memorisation harder. The gap fell from about 8 points to 5.2 while test accuracy rose to 91.8%."},{"n":5,"ti":"Data Augmentation","f":"05-data-augmentation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did Mixup and CutMix lower accuracy in 30-epoch runs?","o":["They make the task harder, so the model needs more epochs; it was still underfitting","They are incompatible with CNNs","They require colour images","They delete the labels"],"a":0,"w":"Training accuracy was only 94.7% and 93.2%. Their reported gains come from schedules of hundreds of epochs."},{"n":5,"ti":"Data Augmentation","f":"05-data-augmentation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"For which dataset is a vertical flip a safe augmentation?","o":["Handwritten digits","Street scenes","Faces","Satellite images"],"a":3,"w":"Overhead images have no canonical up. For digits a flip can change the class; for street scenes and faces it creates unrealistic images."},{"n":5,"ti":"Data Augmentation","f":"05-data-augmentation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On an upside-down test set, the crop + flip model scored 42.5%. The model trained with vertical flips scored…","o":["About the same, 42%","88.6%, close to its normal accuracy","100%","10%"],"a":1,"w":"A model is robust to the transformations it was trained with, and to little else."},{"n":5,"ti":"Data Augmentation","f":"05-data-augmentation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With only 5,000 training images, crop + flip added how much accuracy?","o":["About 1 point","About 3 points","About 11 points","None"],"a":2,"w":"63.4% to 74.7%. With 50,000 images it added 3.4 points: augmentation matters most when data is scarce."},{"n":6,"ti":"Batch Norm & Normalisation Layers","f":"06-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Over which dimensions does BatchNorm compute its mean for a (N, C, H, W) tensor?","o":["C, H and W, per image","H and W only","N, H and W, per channel","All four"],"a":2,"w":"One mean and variance per channel, over the batch and all spatial positions. GroupNorm and LayerNorm instead normalise each image on its own."},{"n":6,"ti":"Batch Norm & Normalisation Layers","f":"06-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A BatchNorm model is evaluated in train mode with batch size 1. What happened to accuracy?","o":["It fell from 89% to about 10%","It stayed at 89%","It rose slightly","It raised an error"],"a":0,"w":"Each image is normalised by its own statistics, removing what distinguishes it. PyTorch only raises an error when a BN layer sees a single value per channel, such as after global pooling."},{"n":6,"ti":"Batch Norm & Normalisation Layers","f":"06-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did a single train-mode pass over class-sorted test batches lower later eval-mode accuracy?","o":["It changed the weights","It changed γ and β","It deleted the running statistics","Train mode updates the running mean and variance, which absorbed the unrepresentative batches"],"a":3,"w":"No gradients flowed, but the running averages moved. Eval accuracy dropped from 89.0% to 77.7% and stayed there."},{"n":6,"ti":"Batch Norm & Normalisation Layers","f":"06-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does folding BatchNorm into the preceding convolution change?","o":["The model’s accuracy","Nothing about the outputs; it removes a layer, so inference is faster","It makes the model train faster","It converts the model to int8"],"a":1,"w":"In eval mode BN is a fixed affine map per channel, so W and b absorb it. Same accuracy within 0.05 points, 7% faster here."},{"n":6,"ti":"Batch Norm & Normalisation Layers","f":"06-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Images in deployment are darker than in training. Which cheap step recovered most of the lost accuracy?","o":["Retraining with labels","Switching to train mode at inference","Re-estimating the BN running statistics on unlabelled darkened images","Increasing the batch size"],"a":2,"w":"85.3% → 88.2%, with no labels and no weight updates. Done on large, shuffled batches, then back to eval mode."},{"n":7,"ti":"Residual Networks","f":"07-residual-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A 56-layer plain CNN has higher training loss than a 20-layer one. What is this?","o":["Overfitting","The degradation problem: deeper plain networks are harder to optimise","Too much weight decay","A data bug"],"a":1,"w":"Overfitting would lower training loss. Here it rose from 0.166 to 0.485 with depth."},{"n":7,"ti":"Residual Networks","f":"07-residual-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"When does a residual block need a 1 × 1 projection on its shortcut?","o":["Always","Only in the first block of the network","When it uses BatchNorm","When the block changes the number of channels or the spatial size"],"a":3,"w":"x and F(x) must have the same shape to be added. A 1 × 1 convolution with the block’s stride fixes both."},{"n":7,"ti":"Residual Networks","f":"07-residual-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What did the gradients of the plain 110-layer network look like at initialisation?","o":["The first layer’s gradient was millions of times larger than the last layer’s","All gradients were zero","Exactly the same as the residual network’s","Smaller at the first layer than the last"],"a":0,"w":"2.7 million against 0.6: an explosion, ratio 4.6 million. The residual-110 ratio was 1.8."},{"n":7,"ti":"Residual Networks","f":"07-residual-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is a bottleneck block cheaper than two 3 × 3 convolutions at 256 channels?","o":["It skips the convolution","It uses a larger stride","The 3 × 3 runs at 64 channels between two 1 × 1 convolutions","It has no BatchNorm"],"a":2,"w":"256·64 + 9·64·64 + 64·256 ≈ 70k multiply-adds per position, against about 1.18 million: 17 times fewer."},{"n":7,"ti":"Residual Networks","f":"07-residual-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With identical training, which scored highest on CIFAR-10 among the 6n + 2 networks?","o":["Plain-20","Residual-110","Plain-110","Residual-20"],"a":1,"w":"Residual-110 reached 91.38%. With skips, depth helped at every step; without them it hurt."},{"n":8,"ti":"Transfer Learning & Fine-Tuning","f":"08-transfer-learning.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does a linear probe train?","o":["Every layer, at a low learning rate","The last stage and the head","Only a linear layer on top of frozen features","Nothing; it uses the ImageNet head"],"a":2,"w":"The backbone is frozen; only a linear classifier on its 2,048-number features is trained. On Pets: 91.06% in 1.9 s."},{"n":8,"ti":"Transfer Learning & Fine-Tuning","f":"08-transfer-learning.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did a ResNet-50 trained from scratch on 3,680 Pets photos reach only 41%?","o":["Far too few images for 23.6 M parameters to learn general visual features from nothing","The learning rate was zero","ResNet-50 cannot classify animals","The images were grayscale"],"a":0,"w":"Pretrained weights bring what 1.28 million ImageNet images taught; from scratch the model must learn edges and textures from 3,680 photos."},{"n":8,"ti":"Transfer Learning & Fine-Tuning","f":"08-transfer-learning.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With two photos per breed (74 images), what did fine-tuning reach on the 37-breed test set?","o":["About 3%, chance","About 41%","About 93%","About 71%"],"a":3,"w":"71.11%; the linear probe reached 57.65%. Chance on 37 classes is 2.7%."},{"n":8,"ti":"Transfer Learning & Fine-Tuning","f":"08-transfer-learning.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does ImageNet transfer so well to Oxford Pets in particular?","o":["Pets is a subset of ImageNet","ImageNet already contains many dog breeds and cats, so its features are close to what the task needs","Pets images are larger","Pets has more classes"],"a":1,"w":"The images differ, but ImageNet’s 1,000 classes include 120 dog breeds and several cats, so fine-grained animal features are already learned."},{"n":8,"ti":"Transfer Learning & Fine-Tuning","f":"08-transfer-learning.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In what order is it usually sensible to try transfer methods?","o":["From scratch, then fine-tune, then probe","Full fine-tune only","Linear probe, then last stage, then full fine-tune","Probe only, never fine-tune"],"a":2,"w":"Each step costs more; the probe is a seconds-long baseline, and fine-tuning is worth it when it beats that baseline."},{"n":9,"ti":"Evaluating Image Classifiers","f":"09-evaluating-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A model’s mean confidence is 96% and its accuracy is 93%. It is…","o":["perfectly calibrated","underconfident","overconfident","overfitting the validation set"],"a":2,"w":"It claims more certainty than it earns. Temperature scaling with T > 1 (1.57 here) softens it."},{"n":9,"ti":"Evaluating Image Classifiers","f":"09-evaluating-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does temperature scaling change?","o":["Only the confidence; every predicted class stays the same","The predicted classes and the accuracy","The model’s weights","The training data"],"a":0,"w":"Dividing logits by a positive T preserves their order, so argmax is unchanged."},{"n":9,"ti":"Evaluating Image Classifiers","f":"09-evaluating-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On which data should the temperature be fitted?","o":["The training set","The test set","CIFAR-10.1","A held-out validation set"],"a":3,"w":"Training data is overfit (too confident); fitting on the test set makes the test score optimistic. Here 5,000 held-out training images were used."},{"n":9,"ti":"Evaluating Image Classifiers","f":"09-evaluating-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The ResNet-18 scored 92.9% on CIFAR-10’s test set. On CIFAR-10.1, built the same way years later, it scored…","o":["About the same","85.2%","50%","Higher"],"a":1,"w":"A 7.7-point drop, in line with published models: a small distribution shift between the two collections."},{"n":9,"ti":"Evaluating Image Classifiers","f":"09-evaluating-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which corruption hurt the model most at its “medium” level?","o":["Brightness +80","Contrast ×0.4","A 3-pixel blur","JPEG quality 20"],"a":2,"w":"Blur 51.65% (and noise σ = 16, 54.39%), against 85.9% for brightness and 83.1% for contrast, which resemble training augmentations."},{"n":10,"ti":"What a CNN Sees: Feature Maps & Grad-CAM","f":"10-what-a-cnn-sees.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can first-layer filters be drawn as images but deeper filters cannot?","o":["Deeper filters are too large","First-layer filters act on RGB pixels; deeper ones act on dozens or hundreds of abstract channels","Deeper filters are random","Only the first layer is trained"],"a":1,"w":"A 7 × 7 × 3 filter is a tiny colour image. A 3 × 3 × 512 filter has no colour interpretation; we visualise what it responds to instead."},{"n":10,"ti":"What a CNN Sees: Feature Maps & Grad-CAM","f":"10-what-a-cnn-sees.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How does Grad-CAM weight each feature map of the last convolutional stage?","o":["Equally","By its largest activation","By the input pixel gradients","By the average gradient of the class score with respect to that map"],"a":3,"w":"The spatially averaged gradient is the channel’s importance for that class; the weighted sum, kept positive, is the heat map."},{"n":10,"ti":"What a CNN Sees: Feature Maps & Grad-CAM","f":"10-what-a-cnn-sees.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is Grad-CAM on ResNet-50 coarse?","o":["It is computed on the 7 × 7 last stage and upsampled","It uses only one channel","It is computed on the first layer","It ignores gradients"],"a":0,"w":"Each of the 49 cells covers a 32-pixel step of the 224 × 224 input (Chapter 2’s jump of 32)."},{"n":10,"ti":"What a CNN Sees: Feature Maps & Grad-CAM","f":"10-what-a-cnn-sees.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"After training with a magenta square on every bird, how many unstamped test birds did the model recognise?","o":["About 80%","About 50%","None","All of them"],"a":2,"w":"0.0%: it had learned the square, not birds. Stamped non-birds were called “bird” 81.9% of the time."},{"n":10,"ti":"What a CNN Sees: Feature Maps & Grad-CAM","f":"10-what-a-cnn-sees.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The shortcut model scored 80.7% overall. Why is that dangerous?","o":["80% is too low to deploy","It looks like an ordinary weaker model, hiding a complete failure on one class","It means the model is overfitting","It proves the data is mislabelled"],"a":1,"w":"Averages hide shortcuts. Per-class results, heat maps and tests that add or remove the cue reveal them."},{"n":11,"ti":"CNN Architectures: VGG to ConvNeXt","f":"11-cnn-architectures.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How much did torchvision’s modern (V2) training recipe add to ResNet-50’s ImageNet top-1?","o":["0.5 points","About 1.5 points","4.8 points","15 points"],"a":2,"w":"76.1% (V1) to 80.9% (V2), with the same architecture: more than separates most architectures in the table."},{"n":11,"ti":"CNN Architectures: VGG to ConvNeXt","f":"11-cnn-architectures.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"VGG-16 has 31 GFLOPs yet was among the fastest models on the GPU at batch 1. Why?","o":["Few, large, regular 3 × 3 layers that tensor cores run at full speed, and few kernel launches","It has the fewest parameters","It skips most of its layers at inference","It runs at lower resolution"],"a":0,"w":"At batch 1 the GPU is underused, so layer count and regularity matter more than FLOPs. On the CPU it was the slowest (42.5 ms)."},{"n":11,"ti":"CNN Architectures: VGG to ConvNeXt","f":"11-cnn-architectures.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is the Pareto front in a cost-versus-accuracy plot?","o":["The most accurate model","The cheapest model","The average of all models","The models for which no other model is both cheaper and more accurate"],"a":3,"w":"Anything off the front is dominated: some other model beats it on both axes, so it is never the right choice for that pair."},{"n":11,"ti":"CNN Architectures: VGG to ConvNeXt","f":"11-cnn-architectures.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which idea did ConvNeXt borrow from vision transformers?","o":["Attention between patches","Large depthwise kernels, LayerNorm, GELU and the AdamW training recipe","A class token","Positional embeddings"],"a":1,"w":"It stays fully convolutional, but adopts transformer-era design choices and training, and reached 82.5% top-1."},{"n":11,"ti":"CNN Architectures: VGG to ConvNeXt","f":"11-cnn-architectures.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which family dominated CPU latency at batch 1 here?","o":["VGG","DenseNet","MobileNet and EfficientNet-B0","ResNet-101"],"a":2,"w":"5.4–6.9 ms on 8 threads, against 15.2 for ResNet-50 and 42.5 for VGG-16: on CPUs, FLOPs translate into time."},{"n":12,"ti":"Efficient Convolutions","f":"12-efficient-convolutions.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How many weights does a depthwise 3 × 3 convolution on 64 channels have?","o":["36,864","576","4,096","64"],"a":1,"w":"One 3 × 3 kernel per channel: 9 × 64. A standard 3 × 3 from 64 to 64 channels has 9 × 64 × 64 = 36,864."},{"n":12,"ti":"Efficient Convolutions","f":"12-efficient-convolutions.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A depthwise layer did 64× fewer FLOPs than a standard one but ran only 3× faster on the GPU. Why?","o":["The GPU does not support depthwise convolutions","bf16 was not used","The FLOP count was wrong","It is memory-bound: little arithmetic per byte read and written, so bandwidth sets the time"],"a":3,"w":"9 multiply-adds per output cannot keep tensor cores busy; the layer ran at 3.1 TFLOPS against 62.5 for the dense one."},{"n":12,"ti":"Efficient Convolutions","f":"12-efficient-convolutions.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Where did MobileNet’s FLOP savings turn into the biggest speed-up over ResNet-18?","o":["On one CPU thread at batch 1","On the GPU at batch 1","On the GPU at batch 64","Nowhere"],"a":0,"w":"9.5 ms against 27.6 ms on one core (2.9×). On the GPU at batch 1 MobileNet v2 was 2.3× slower."},{"n":12,"ti":"Efficient Convolutions","f":"12-efficient-convolutions.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is “inverted” about MobileNet v2’s inverted residual?","o":["It subtracts the input instead of adding it","It runs backwards","It goes narrow → wide → narrow, the opposite of ResNet’s wide → narrow → wide bottleneck","It has no activation"],"a":2,"w":"A 1 × 1 expands channels (×6), a depthwise 3 × 3 filters the wide tensor, a 1 × 1 projects back; the residual joins the narrow ends."},{"n":12,"ti":"Efficient Convolutions","f":"12-efficient-convolutions.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is the right way to choose between two architectures for a phone app?","o":["Pick the one with fewer FLOPs","Measure latency on the target phone at the real batch size, alongside accuracy","Pick the one with fewer parameters","Benchmark both on a server GPU"],"a":1,"w":"Rankings changed between GPU and CPU and between batch sizes here. Only measurement on the target settles it."},{"n":13,"ti":"Detection Basics: Boxes, IoU, Anchors & NMS","f":"13-detection-basics.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Two equal boxes overlap by half their width. What is their IoU?","o":["0.5","1/3","0.25","2/3"],"a":1,"w":"Intersection is half a box; the union is one and a half boxes: 0.5 / 1.5 = 1/3."},{"n":13,"ti":"Detection Basics: Boxes, IoU, Anchors & NMS","f":"13-detection-basics.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is predicting log(w_gt / w_anchor) better than predicting the width in pixels?","o":["It is a small, scale-independent number the same head can predict at every position","It is faster to compute","It removes the need for NMS","It makes IoU larger"],"a":0,"w":"Relative, log-scaled targets are near zero for good anchors whatever the object’s size, which makes regression much easier to learn."},{"n":13,"ti":"Detection Basics: Boxes, IoU, Anchors & NMS","f":"13-detection-basics.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What happened to person AP when NMS was switched off in Faster R-CNN?","o":["It rose slightly","It stayed about the same","It fell to zero","It fell from about 60.6 to 18.4"],"a":3,"w":"The top 100 boxes per image were mostly duplicates, each counted as a false positive."},{"n":13,"ti":"Detection Basics: Boxes, IoU, Anchors & NMS","f":"13-detection-basics.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why use batched_nms instead of one NMS over all boxes?","o":["It is more accurate within a class","It sorts boxes differently","So boxes of different classes never suppress each other","It avoids computing IoU"],"a":2,"w":"A person on a bicycle has heavily overlapping boxes of two classes; both should survive."},{"n":13,"ti":"Detection Basics: Boxes, IoU, Anchors & NMS","f":"13-detection-basics.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"With one 128-pixel square anchor size, what share of COCO objects had an anchor at IoU ≥ 0.5?","o":["86%","About 15%","50%","100%"],"a":1,"w":"14.6%. Five sizes on FPN levels with three aspect ratios raised it to 86.1%."},{"n":14,"ti":"Two-Stage Detectors: Faster R-CNN","f":"14-two-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does Faster R-CNN’s region proposal network output?","o":["Final class labels for every object","A segmentation mask","About 1,000 class-agnostic candidate boxes with objectness scores","One box per anchor, all kept"],"a":2,"w":"It scores every anchor as object or background, refines the boxes, applies NMS and keeps the top proposals for the second stage."},{"n":14,"ti":"Two-Stage Detectors: Faster R-CNN","f":"14-two-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"From which FPN level is a 56 × 56-pixel region pooled?","o":["P2 (stride 4)","P3 (stride 8)","P4 (stride 16)","P5 (stride 32)"],"a":0,"w":"k = ⌊4 + log₂(56/224)⌋ = ⌊4 − 2⌋ = 2. Small regions use the finest map."},{"n":14,"ti":"Two-Stage Detectors: Faster R-CNN","f":"14-two-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does RoI Align beat RoI Pool?","o":["It uses more bins","It is faster","It uses average instead of max pooling","It avoids rounding the box and bins to the grid, sampling exact positions bilinearly"],"a":3,"w":"Measured: 21.6 px mean misplacement for RoI Pool, 0.0 for RoI Align on a linear feature map."},{"n":14,"ti":"Two-Stage Detectors: Faster R-CNN","f":"14-two-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Where did most of Faster R-CNN v2’s 32 ms per image go?","o":["Resizing the image","About 14 ms in the backbone and FPN, the rest in the RPN and RoI heads","Almost all in NMS","Copying to the GPU"],"a":1,"w":"Backbone + FPN 13.6 ms, RPN 8.1, RoI heads 9.6: the two-stage machinery cost more than the backbone."},{"n":14,"ti":"Two-Stage Detectors: Faster R-CNN","f":"14-two-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Fine-tuned on 120 pedestrian photos, which start gave 99% AP50 after one epoch?","o":["Random weights","An ImageNet backbone with new heads","The full COCO-trained detector with a new box predictor","None reached 99%"],"a":2,"w":"The COCO detector already detects people. From an ImageNet backbone only, it reached 46.1 after ten epochs."},{"n":15,"ti":"One-Stage Detectors: YOLO, RetinaNet & Anchor-Free","f":"15-one-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"With plain cross-entropy, where did most of RetinaNet’s classification loss come from?","o":["Foreground anchors","Easy background anchors, by sheer number","Ignored anchors","The box regression"],"a":1,"w":"85.3%: each easy background anchor has a tiny loss, but there were 8.4 million of them in 50 images."},{"n":15,"ti":"One-Stage Detectors: YOLO, RetinaNet & Anchor-Free","f":"15-one-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"With γ = 2, how much less does an example with p_t = 0.9 contribute under focal loss than under cross-entropy?","o":["2 times","10 times","It contributes more","100 times"],"a":3,"w":"(1 − 0.9)² = 0.01: a hundredth of its cross-entropy."},{"n":15,"ti":"One-Stage Detectors: YOLO, RetinaNet & Anchor-Free","f":"15-one-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is RetinaNet’s classification bias initialised so that p = 0.01?","o":["So millions of background anchors do not produce a huge loss in the first iterations","To make the model predict more objects","Because focal loss requires it mathematically","To speed up inference"],"a":0,"w":"At p = 0.5 every background anchor would start with a large loss, destabilising early training."},{"n":15,"ti":"One-Stage Detectors: YOLO, RetinaNet & Anchor-Free","f":"15-one-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does an FCOS location regress instead of anchor offsets?","o":["A heat map","The box’s area","The four distances to the box’s left, top, right and bottom edges","Nothing; it only classifies"],"a":2,"w":"Plus a centre-ness score that down-weights predictions from near the box edges."},{"n":15,"ti":"One-Stage Detectors: YOLO, RetinaNet & Anchor-Free","f":"15-one-stage-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why did SSDlite score only 2.5 AP on small objects?","o":["It has no box regression","It works on 320-pixel inputs, so small objects are only a few pixels across","It uses focal loss","It was trained on a different dataset"],"a":1,"w":"Resizing a 640-pixel image to 320 halves every object; a 20-pixel object becomes 10 pixels, below what its features resolve."},{"n":16,"ti":"Evaluating Detectors: mAP from Scratch","f":"16-evaluating-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A second detection of an object that has already been matched is counted as…","o":["a true positive","ignored","a false positive","a false negative"],"a":2,"w":"Each ground-truth box can be matched once; duplicates lower precision, which is why NMS matters for AP."},{"n":16,"ti":"Evaluating Detectors: mAP from Scratch","f":"16-evaluating-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does COCO’s AP@[.5:.95] average over?","o":["Ten IoU thresholds from 0.50 to 0.95, and over classes","Ten score thresholds","Ten random subsets of images","Precision at ten recall levels only"],"a":0,"w":"Each threshold gets its own 101-point AP per class; the reported AP is the mean of all of them."},{"n":16,"ti":"Evaluating Detectors: mAP from Scratch","f":"16-evaluating-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does interpolating precision in the PR curve do?","o":["Averages precision over images","Removes false positives","Smooths the curve with a moving average","Replaces precision at each recall by the best precision at that recall or higher"],"a":3,"w":"It makes the curve non-increasing, so AP does not depend on small wiggles in the ranking."},{"n":16,"ti":"Evaluating Detectors: mAP from Scratch","f":"16-evaluating-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Keeping only detections scoring above 0.5 changed Faster R-CNN’s AP how?","o":["Raised it, by removing false positives","Lowered it from 51.3 to 49.0, by cutting off the high-recall end of the curve","No change","Lowered it to about 20"],"a":1,"w":"AP integrates over all thresholds; truncating the list removes recall it could have earned."},{"n":16,"ti":"Evaluating Detectors: mAP from Scratch","f":"16-evaluating-detectors.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How are detections that overlap a crowd (iscrowd) region treated by COCO evaluation?","o":["As false positives","As true positives","Ignored: neither rewarded nor penalised","Counted twice"],"a":2,"w":"A crowd region marks many unannotated people; detections inside it are ignored, and one crowd region can absorb several."},{"n":40,"ti":"The CV Interview Playbook","f":"40-interview-playbook.html","lv":"Expert","q":"What is the output size of a 3 × 3 convolution with stride 2 and padding 1 on a 56 × 56 input?","o":["27","28","29","56"],"a":1,"w":"(56 + 2 − 2 − 1) // 2 + 1 = 27 + 1 = 28."},{"n":40,"ti":"The CV Interview Playbook","f":"40-interview-playbook.html","lv":"Expert","q":"How many parameters does a 3 × 3 convolution from 64 to 128 channels have, without bias?","o":["1,152","8,192","36,864","73,728"],"a":3,"w":"128 × 64 × 3 × 3. Forgetting the 64 input channels gives 1,152."},{"n":40,"ti":"The CV Interview Playbook","f":"40-interview-playbook.html","lv":"Expert","q":"How many tokens does ViT-B/16 process for a 384 × 384 image?","o":["577","197","576","1,025"],"a":0,"w":"(384/16)² = 576 patches plus the class token = 577; at 224 it is 197."},{"n":40,"ti":"The CV Interview Playbook","f":"40-interview-playbook.html","lv":"Expert","q":"In a debugging round, what should usually come before retraining?","o":["Trying a bigger model","Collecting more data","Checking the inputs the model actually receives, and its mode","Changing the loss"],"a":2,"w":"Pipeline bugs (channel order, normalisation, eval mode, orientation) are common, cheap to test and invisible to retraining."},{"n":40,"ti":"The CV Interview Playbook","f":"40-interview-playbook.html","lv":"Expert","q":"What should the first minutes of a design round be spent on?","o":["Choosing the backbone","Requirements: the task, the metric, latency, hardware and labels","Writing the training loop","Drawing the network"],"a":1,"w":"Requirements decide the model, the data and the evaluation. A design without a metric cannot be judged."}];
